  "aws.s3.storage-classes": {
   "id": "aws.s3.storage-classes",
   "kind": "concept",
   "front": "Which S3 storage class fits which access pattern?",
   "back": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Standard</strong> — frequent reads, milliseconds</li>\n<li><strong>Intelligent-Tiering</strong> — unknown or changing pattern; objects move between tiers automatically</li>\n<li><strong>Standard-IA</strong> — infrequent reads, milliseconds; retrieval fee, 30-day minimum</li>\n<li><strong>One Zone-IA</strong> — like Standard-IA in one AZ; only for data you can re-create</li>\n<li><strong>Glacier Instant Retrieval</strong> — read about once a quarter, still milliseconds</li>\n<li><strong>Glacier Flexible Retrieval</strong> — minutes to hours to restore</li>\n<li><strong>Glacier Deep Archive</strong> — cheapest; restore within 12 h (bulk 48 h)</li>\n</ul>",
   "image": "data:image/svg+xml;base64,",
   "tags": [
    "service:s3",
    "domain:cost"
   ],
   "examples": [],
   "related": []
  },
  "aws.s3.intelligent-tiering": {
   "id": "aws.s3.intelligent-tiering",
   "kind": "concept",
   "front": "When should you choose S3 Intelligent-Tiering?",
   "back": "<p>When the access pattern is <strong>unknown or changes over time</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>S3 moves each object between tiers by itself: Frequent → Infrequent after 30 days without access → Archive Instant after 90 days.</li>\n<li><strong>No retrieval fees</strong>; you pay a small monthly monitoring fee per object.</li>\n<li>Objects under 128 KB are not monitored and stay billed at the frequent tier.</li>\n</ul>",
   "tags": [
    "service:s3",
    "domain:cost"
   ],
   "examples": [],
   "related": [
    "aws.s3.storage-classes"
   ]
  },
  "aws.s3.lifecycle-rules": {
   "id": "aws.s3.lifecycle-rules",
   "kind": "concept",
   "front": "What can S3 lifecycle rules do?",
   "back": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Transition</strong> objects to colder classes after N days (per bucket, prefix or tag)</li>\n<li><strong>Expire</strong> objects or noncurrent versions after N days</li>\n<li><strong>Abort incomplete multipart uploads</strong></li>\n<li>Objects must stay <strong>30 days</strong> in Standard before moving to Standard-IA or One Zone-IA</li>\n</ul>",
   "tags": [
    "service:s3",
    "domain:cost"
   ],
   "examples": [],
   "related": [
    "aws.s3.storage-classes"
   ]
  },
  "aws.s3.versioning": {
   "id": "aws.s3.versioning",
   "kind": "concept",
   "front": "What does S3 Versioning protect against, and what are its rules?",
   "back": "<ul>\n<li>Keeps <strong>every version</strong> of an object: an overwrite keeps the old version, a delete only adds a <em>delete marker</em>.</li>\n<li>Once enabled, versioning can be <strong>suspended</strong> but never turned fully off.</li>\n<li>Required for <strong>replication</strong> and for <strong>MFA Delete</strong>.</li>\n<li>Pair it with a lifecycle rule that expires noncurrent versions to control cost.</li>\n</ul>",
   "tags": [
    "service:s3",
    "domain:resilient"
   ],
   "examples": [],
   "related": []
  },
  "aws.s3.replication": {
   "id": "aws.s3.replication",
   "kind": "concept",
   "front": "S3 Cross-Region Replication (CRR) — what must be in place, and what does it copy?",
   "back": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Versioning</strong> must be enabled on both the source and the destination bucket, plus an IAM role S3 can use.</li>\n<li>Replicates objects written <strong>after</strong> the rule exists; use <strong>S3 Batch Replication</strong> for existing objects.</li>\n<li>Works across accounts and can store replicas in a different storage class.</li>\n<li>Use cases: compliance copies far away, lower latency for distant users, disaster recovery.</li>\n<li><strong>Same-Region Replication (SRR)</strong>: the same within one region (log aggregation, prod → test copies).</li>\n</ul>",
   "tags": [
    "service:s3",
    "domain:resilient"
   ],
   "examples": [],
   "related": [
    "aws.s3.versioning"
   ]
  },
  "aws.s3.block-public-access": {
   "id": "aws.s3.block-public-access",
   "kind": "concept",
   "front": "What does S3 Block Public Access do?",
   "back": "<ul>\n<li>An account- or bucket-level guardrail that <strong>overrides</strong> bucket policies and ACLs that would grant public access.</li>\n<li><strong>On by default</strong> for new buckets.</li>\n<li>To serve public content, keep the bucket private and put <strong>CloudFront with Origin Access Control (OAC)</strong> in front of it.</li>\n</ul>",
   "tags": [
    "service:s3",
    "domain:secure"
   ],
   "examples": [],
   "related": []
  },
  "aws.s3.encryption": {
   "id": "aws.s3.encryption",
   "kind": "concept",
   "front": "Which S3 encryption option fits which requirement?",
   "back": "<ul>\n<li><strong>SSE-S3</strong> — S3 manages the keys; the default for every new object</li>\n<li><strong>SSE-KMS</strong> — keys in AWS KMS: key policies, <strong>CloudTrail audit</strong> of every key use, KMS request quotas apply</li>\n<li><strong>DSSE-KMS</strong> — two layers of KMS encryption for strict compliance</li>\n<li><strong>SSE-C</strong> — you send your own key with every request (HTTPS only); S3 never stores it</li>\n<li><strong>Client-side</strong> — encrypt before the data leaves your application</li>\n</ul>",
   "tags": [
    "service:s3",
    "domain:secure"
   ],
   "examples": [],
   "related": []
  },
  "aws.ec2.purchasing-options": {
   "id": "aws.ec2.purchasing-options",
   "kind": "concept",
   "front": "EC2 purchasing options — which one when?",
   "back": "<ul>\n<li><strong>On-Demand</strong> — no commitment; short or unpredictable workloads that must not be interrupted</li>\n<li><strong>Savings Plans</strong> — commit to a $/hour spend for 1 or 3 years; Compute Savings Plans also cover Fargate and Lambda</li>\n<li><strong>Reserved Instances</strong> — 1 or 3 years for a specific instance type in a region; steady workloads</li>\n<li><strong>Spot</strong> — up to 90% off, but can be reclaimed with a <strong>2-minute warning</strong>; fault-tolerant, flexible jobs</li>\n<li><strong>Dedicated Hosts</strong> — a whole physical server; per-socket / per-core licenses, compliance</li>\n</ul>",
   "tags": [
    "service:ec2",
    "domain:cost"
   ],
   "examples": [],
   "related": []
  },
  "aws.ec2.auto-scaling": {
   "id": "aws.ec2.auto-scaling",
   "kind": "concept",
   "front": "How does EC2 Auto Scaling keep an application available and right-sized?",
   "back": "<ul>\n<li>An <strong>Auto Scaling group</strong> spreads instances across several AZs within min / desired / max capacity.</li>\n<li><strong>Health checks</strong> (EC2 or ELB) replace unhealthy instances automatically.</li>\n<li>Scaling policies: <strong>target tracking</strong> (e.g. keep average CPU at 50%), step, scheduled, predictive.</li>\n</ul>",
   "tags": [
    "service:ec2",
    "domain:resilient"
   ],
   "examples": [],
   "related": []
  },
  "aws.ec2.placement-groups": {
   "id": "aws.ec2.placement-groups",
   "kind": "concept",
   "front": "EC2 placement groups: cluster, spread, partition",
   "back": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Cluster</strong> — instances close together in one AZ: lowest latency, highest throughput (HPC)</li>\n<li><strong>Spread</strong> — each instance on distinct hardware, max 7 running instances per AZ per group (a few critical instances)</li>\n<li><strong>Partition</strong> — groups of instances on separate racks, up to 7 partitions per AZ (HDFS, Cassandra, Kafka)</li>\n</ul>",
   "tags": [
    "service:ec2",
    "domain:performance"
   ],
   "examples": [],
   "related": []
  },
